{"workflow_type":"norn_fleet","workflow_id":"09365786-1fef-42b6-b8a4-1aee9364894b","run_id":"1e40b586-665b-4fb5-8ea9-39b968f6259b","result":{"outcome":"done","payload":{"attempted":2,"issue_ids":["issue-153","issue-151"],"verdicts":["VERDICT: CONFIRMED\n\nEVIDENCE: `crates/aion-server/src/awl/documents.rs:264-272` permits an underscore as the first character: `character.is_ascii_lowercase() || character == '_'`; subsequent characters may be lowercase letters, digits, or underscores. Conversely, `crates/aion-awl/src/lexer/cursor.rs:99-105` dispatches identifiers only when `ch.is_ascii_lowercase()` (or uppercase for type identifiers); anything else—including leading `_`—produces `stray character`. Identifier continuation permits underscores only after the first character: `ch.is_ascii_alphanumeric() || ch == '_'` at `cursor.rs:256-258`.\n\nCAUSE: The server validator treats `_` as both an identifier-start and identifier-continuation character, while the AWL lexer treats it only as an identifier-continuation character. Thus `_workflow` passes `validate_document_name`, but the generated `workflow _workflow` source cannot be lexed. `new_document_source` subsequently parses the generated source, so creation still fails, but only after contradictory validation has accepted the name.\n\nFIX: Move the server rule to match AWL rather than broadening the lexer, because the lexer, checker, and other identifier validation already consistently require an initial lowercase letter. In `validate_document_name`, change the first-character predicate to `.is_some_and(|character| character.is_ascii_lowercase())`, retaining lowercase letters, digits, and underscores for continuation. Add server tests proving `validate_document_name(\"_workflow\")` and `create(..., name: \"_workflow\")` return `DocumentError::InvalidName` without creating a file, while `first_workflow` remains accepted. Add or retain an `aion-awl/tests/lexer.rs` regression asserting `_workflow` produces a leading-underscore/stray-character lexing error.","VERDICT: CONFIRMED\n\nEVIDENCE: `crates/aion/src/runtime/outcome.rs:101-117` passes a Beam runtime `Pid` into `unwrap_gleam_result` and constructs `message: format!(\"workflow {pid} returned error\")`; thus `2` is a process identifier, not a workflow/run identifier. `crates/aion-client/src/ops.rs:133-145` decodes `response.workflow_id` and `response.run_id` from the start response and stores both in `WorkflowHandle`. `crates/aion-cli/src/one_motion.rs:168-175` extracts those identifiers before polling. At `crates/aion-cli/src/one_motion.rs:191-193`, the failure branch nevertheless emits only `workflow run failed: {render_failure(error)}`. `crates/aion-cli/src/one_motion.rs:228-240` renders the runtime message and optional `WorkflowError.details`, but accepts neither identifier. Existing `crates/aion-cli/tests/one_motion_live_e2e.rs:283-293` even documents the process ordinal as unactionable without asserting workflow/run IDs.\n\nCAUSE: The runtime correctly records a low-level process-oriented failure message, but the CLI treats `WorkflowError::Display` as the operator-facing identity. The actionable IDs are already available in `await_result` through the start response-backed handle but are omitted from the terminal failure arm. Strictly, they are not fields inside `WorkflowError.details`; that payload contains the routed AWL failure details. They are nevertheless in hand at exactly the rendering point.\n\nFIX: Fix this at the CLI presentation layer, preserving runtime diagnostics: change the `WorkflowFailed` arm to append `\\n  workflow_id: {workflow_id}\\n  run_id: {run_id}` after `render_failure(error)` (or pass both IDs into that renderer). Add a focused renderer/unit test using fixed IDs, and extend `one_motion_run_reports_a_failure_outcome_with_its_details_and_exits_nonzero` to assert stderr contains both labels and usable IDs, while retaining its AWL outcome/detail assertions."],"control_reply":"CLAUDE.md"}}}
